    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>SystÃ¨me de cote ÃnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="5" build="8">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.5b8</English>
                <French>v15.5b8</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16</English>
                <French>v1.16</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <mainWalls>Upgrade the walls by a minimum of R-20. If you are adding insulation to the exterior of the home, I would recommend using EPS rigid board insulation as it allows water vapor to pass through it. This is crucial for letting the walls dry out properly and will avoid moisture issues. You could also use a rock wool insulation as well.</mainWalls>
                <windows>Upgrade the windows to ones rated for Energy Star Zone 3.</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>If you are doing any major renovations such as adding insulation to the walls, you will be decreasing the amount of natural air leakage into the home. This is great for the amount of heat loss, but the amount of air that was entering the home will be decreased and the house will need proper ventilation. A HRV will supply fresh heated air into the home as it exhausts stale air to the exterior. These units are quite efficient as they collect heat from the exhaust air and it is used to heat the supply air without mixing. This is done through a heat transfer device inside of the unit. </VentilationSystem>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
